.TH "TAKO" "1" "Jun 2026" "Tako CLI" "Tako CLI Manual"

.SH NAME
tako-heal-status - Show self-healing state, pauses, breaker and journal


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtako heal status [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Show the self-healing state that takod reports on every server of the
environment: per-service state and skip reason, pauses, the node circuit
breaker and the recent decision journal.

.PP
Exit codes: 0 ok or disabled; 6 a service gave up, a breaker is open or a node
could not be read; 4 every node unreachable; 2 no node supports
node.self-healing-v1 (run tako upgrade servers).


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for status

.PP
\fB--journal\fP=20
	Recent journal entries per node (0-1000)

.PP
\fB--server\fP=""
	One server only

.PP
\fB-s\fP, \fB--service\fP=""
	One service only


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--config\fP=""
	config file (default is ./tako.yaml or ./tako.json)

.PP
\fB-e\fP, \fB--env\fP=""
	environment to use (default: the only environment, else production, else the first alphabetically)

.PP
\fB--events\fP=""
	stream progress events to stdout: ndjson (human output moves to stderr)

.PP
\fB--host-key-mode\fP=""
	SSH host key verification mode: tofu, strict, ask (default: tofu)

.PP
\fB--output\fP="text"
	output format: text or json (json reserves stdout for the final result document)

.PP
\fB--preview\fP=""
	preview environment to use (deploy, ps, logs, exec, history, rollback, config explain); -e then names its base

.PP
\fB-v\fP, \fB--verbose\fP[=false]
	verbose output


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBtako-heal(1)\fP
